# opengrep / semgrep ignore — gate 4 (SAST). Copy to the repo ROOT as
# `.semgrepignore`, then delete any entry your repo does not have.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE IS MANDATORY, not optional ────────────────────────────────
#
# If NO `.semgrepignore` exists in the scanned root, the engine falls back to a
# BUILT-IN ignore template that drops your test code. Nobody in this fleet chose
# that. Measured 2026-08-11 on the pinned CI engine (opengrep v1.22.0 CLI on
# Linux — the exact build gate 4 installs), with a controlled detector and one
# variable changed at a time:
#
#   file                       content                                result
#   ─────────────────────────  ─────────────────────────────────────  ────────
#   src/inline.go              db.Query(fmt.Sprintf(...))             FIRES
#   tests/inline.go            byte-identical to the file above       SILENT
#
# `paths.scanned` listed 3 of 4 files and `paths.skipped` reported `none`, so
# NOTHING in the gate output tells you a test tree was dropped. Pointing the
# scanner directly at `tests/` returns `paths.scanned: []` — an explicit path
# argument does NOT override it.
#
# EXACTLY WHAT THE BUILT-IN TEMPLATE DROPS, enumerated by scanning one identical
# vulnerable file per candidate path with and without a `.semgrepignore`:
#
#   DROPPED                       KEPT (for contrast — the set is narrower
#   ────────────────────────────  than "anything that looks like a test")
#   engine_test.go                test_app.py
#   pkg/engine_test.go            app_test.py
#   test/helper.go                conftest.py
#   tests/helper.go               spec/thing.py
#   tests/test_app.py             src/app.test.ts
#                                 src/app.spec.ts
#                                 __tests__/app.ts
#                                 e2e/app.ts
#                                 testdata/sample.go
#
# So the rule is: `*_test.go` AT ANY DEPTH, plus the `test/` and `tests/`
# directories. Python and TypeScript test FILENAMES are not excluded — only the
# two directories are. That asymmetry matters: a Go repo following the language's
# own `foo_test.go` convention has its ENTIRE test suite dropped no matter where
# the files sit, which is why DevExtreme-Filter-Go-Language lost 49 files.
#
# The engine drops the built-in template as soon as ANY `.semgrepignore` exists,
# so merely shipping this file restores the test code; the entries below are then
# a DELIBERATE choice you can review.
#
# Blast radius when adopted, measured against real clones with each caller's own
# `opengrep-paths`: +49 newly-visible files in DevExtreme-Filter-Go-Language
# (its entire `_test.go` suite, which gate 4 had never once looked at), and
# ZERO new findings in 10 of the 11 public repos that ship `.quality/opengrep`.
#
# ── ANCHORING: `**/vendor/` ALONE IS A SILENT LEAK ──────────────────────────
#
# A `**/`-prefixed entry does NOT match the ROOT-level directory of that name.
# Measured with a positive control — three identical vulnerable files:
#
#   entry            rule fired on
#   ───────────────  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   (none)           src/a.go, vendor/pkg/b.go, app/vendor/pkg/c.go
#   `**/vendor/`     src/a.go, vendor/pkg/b.go        <-- root vendor LEAKED
#   `vendor/`        src/a.go                          <-- both trees excluded
#
# There is no warning on stderr when this happens. So every directory exclusion
# below ships BOTH the root-anchored and the nested form.
#
# ── RULES FOR EDITING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. NEVER add `test/`, `tests/`, `spec/` or `__tests__/`. First-party test code
#    is first-party code: it handles credentials, builds queries, and shells
#    out. Re-excluding it restores the exact hole this file closes.
# 2. Keep the list narrow and greppable, and give every entry a written reason.
# 3. Do not use this file to make a finding go away. An exclusion is a statement
#    about SCOPE ("this is not our source"), never about severity.

.git/

# Vendored third-party source. Not ours to fix, and upstream style trips
# first-party rules. Secrets in these trees are still covered by gitleaks
# (gate 5), which scans them independently of this file.
vendor/
**/vendor/
vendor_src/
**/vendor_src/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Installed dependencies (virtualenvs). Third-party bytes, not source.
.venv/
**/.venv/
venv/
**/venv/

# Build output — generated from source that gate 4 already scans, so scanning
# it again only duplicates findings against files nobody edits.
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/
out/
**/out/
